{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_107","type":"agent_message","text":"Built [**UFOh Orbit — play the single HTML file**](/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.build-v2__20261106-034456__x8d70ab/work/UFOh-Orbit-UFO-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-034456.html).\n- [Exit interview](/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.build-v2__20261106-034456__x8d70ab/work/exit-interview.json)\n- [Progress record](/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.build-v2__20261106-034456__x8d70ab/work/PROGRESS.md)\n\n**420 checks passed, 0 failed.** Includes the complete mission, score attack, couch co-op, AI Hub multiplayer, touch controls, attract mode, and Dev Mode.\n\n**Source Information:** Date: 2026-11-06 03:44:56. Based on [UFO! (Odyssey², 1981)](https://odyssey2.info/db/game/ufo-49), following PLAN.md. Fixed overhead arcade shooter using JavaScript, Canvas2D, Web Audio, my-3D2dge 0.89.1, Hub client 1.4.0, and esbuild 0.25.12. Native procedural pixel art, fixed-step animation, tweens, easing, and paths. Original synthesized music, effects, and gibberish voices; music defaults to 10%. Supports 1–2 players locally or through Hub. Research covered the manual, reviews, and reference images; historical audio remains unknown. Testing used headless automation and device stand-ins. Environment: Codex CLI, Linux. Model: OpenAI gpt-6.1-sol, ultra. Usage: **added on import**.\n\n**Testing Feedback:** Eight tested defects fixed; two critical defects fixed overall. Most helpful: complete legal-input mission replay. Least helpful: static packaging assertions. Physical devices and human listening were unavailable.\n\n**Engine Feedback:** Best features: native pixels, palettes, menus, and synth. Wrong assumptions involved boot behavior and drawing layers. The main custom system was authoritative combat and co-op simulation. Fixed arena projection and network authority were hardest. Engine changes: none. Suggested improvement: a simultaneous shared-world arcade starter.\n\n**Prompt Feedback:** Concrete mechanics and verification requirements helped most. Starter defaults conflicted with fixed bounds and simultaneous players. Integrating combat, controls, and remote authority was hardest. Suggested change: prioritize acceptance checks and consolidate the delivery contract."}}
